## THE RATE DROP IS NOW FULLY EXPLAINED, AND IT CORRECTS BOTH OF MY EARLIER ACCOUNTS.
The log carries a line type I had not seen until this read: 'COOLDOWN bbg: waiting 897 s before the
next job', logged as a countdown every 30 s (898, 868, 838 ... ), so ~30 LINES per ONE wait.
  - TODAY'S EPISODES: 4, each opening at 897 s -- 08:07:30Z, 08:50:13Z, 10:16:13Z, 10:34:32Z.
    4 x ~15 min = about an hour of deliberate waiting, which is exactly the 21/hour -> 15/hour drop.
  - WHAT TRIGGERS ONE: the job immediately before each episode reads status=error. The 08:07:28Z line
    I called 'one long job' last read is in fact 'JOB bbg security-0f3eb2ded85ad44b status=error',
    followed 2 seconds later by the cooldown. So the gap was BACKOFF, not a slow fetch.
  - AND IT IS PRE-EXISTING, which is the arm that decides whether this is a finding: status=error per
    day over the whole log reads 6 (10-10), 11 (10-11), 4 (10-12), 1 (10-13), 4 (10-14), and cooldown
    episodes per day read 2, 11, 4, -, 4. Errors and episodes track one for one on every day that has
    both. Today's 4 is inside the historical range and the mechanism predates the new pin by days.
  - DENOMINATOR for the status census: 1531 JOB lines total -- 1472 no_new_articles, 30 error, 14
    new_articles, 12 sign_in_refused, 4 no_acceptable_new_articles, 4 empty, 3 bot_check.
SO: no row. Nothing in bz-10 or bz-11 claims zero errored jobs, the backoff is the runner's own
designed behaviour, and bz-10's R4 (no AttributeError/OSError/WinError line) remains true -- a job
status=error is a different signal from a python traceback, and conflating them would have been the
mistake in the other direction.
  Remaining 35 of the expected 242. At the measured 15/hour that is about 2.3 hours; the honest range
  is 1.6 to 2.3 hours depending on how many more errored jobs draw a cooldown.

